write a personalised 1-week home workout plan for a client, based on their onboarding details below. Write it the way a real coach would send it — clear and structured where it needs to be (exercises, sets, reps), but with a personal, conversational voice around it, not like an auto-generated template.

CLIENT DETAILS:
[paste client details here]

HOW TO WRITE IT:

1. Start with a short personal note (2-4 sentences) addressing the client by name — reference their goal, available equipment (bodyweight only, dumbbells, resistance bands, etc.), and space constraints if mentioned. Keep it casual, like a coach checking in.

2. Opening mesocycle (month 1, week 1): pick a home split that fits THIS client (goal, space, equipment, injuries, how long they have trained). Proven templates are fine (full body, upper/lower, full-body circuits) when they match the days and duration. Personalise exercise selection; do not invent unsafe novelty just to be unique. Name each day as "Day N (Weekday): Focus" where Day 1 is Monday through Day 7 Sunday (e.g. "Day 1 (Monday): Lower Power"). Never use bare "Day N" alone or weekday-only headers. Use BASE (lowest) volume for week 1: 2 to 3 working sets per exercise, about 5 to 7 working exercises per session, never 5+ working sets, and leave reps in reserve on hard moves. Do not write calorie targets in the workout; food lives in the diet plan. Never use hyphen, en dash, or em dash characters in client-facing text.

3. Choose exercises realistically based on the client's stated equipment:
   - Bodyweight only → use progressions/regressions for difficulty (e.g. incline pushups, deficit pushups, single-leg variations, tempo work, pause reps) since loading options are limited
   - If they have dumbbells/resistance bands/kettlebell → incorporate them for the major lifts where useful (rows, presses, squats, RDLs)
   - Reps by experience: beginners 8 to 15 on compounds. Intermediate 6 to 12. For home "compound-style" movements (squats, hinges, presses, rows) when heavy loading isn't available: 6 to 12 with tempo/pause. Accessories: 10 to 20.
   - Default 2 to 3 working sets. Never 5+ working sets. 4 working sets only on one main move for advanced clients.
   - **NEVER prescribe barbell, smith machine, leg press, cable machines, or commercial gym equipment unless explicitly listed in available equipment. Band exercises must be clearly labeled as band/resistance band moves.**

4. Include 1 core finisher per training day (2 max on a dedicated core day). Do not add three or four extra ab circuits on every session. Never include exercises the client listed as dislikes.

5. Adjust exercises for any limitations the client mentioned (e.g. knee issues → avoid jump-heavy or deep-flexion moves, no overhead pressing → swap for floor press variations).

6. Open the plan with a short warmup routine (around 10 minutes) — light cardio (jumping jacks, jog in place, skipping if space allows), joint mobility, and a few activation movements — written as a simple flowing list rather than a rigid numbered table.

7. For each training day, list exercises with sets/reps clearly using "Exercise: N sets x M reps" (plain letter x). Add a short coaching note here and there — a cue on form, tempo, what to focus on without equipment, written like a coach's aside rather than a formal instruction block.
   Name lifts in ordinary coach English with the tool when it matters (Dumbbell Romanian Deadlift, Resistance Band Row, Incline Push Up). One movement per line. No nicknames, no two lifts in one name. Do not copy a vendor catalog if that would break equipment or injury constraints.
   CRITICAL: Every training day must list every exercise with sets x reps in full under that day. NEVER write "same as Day 1", "repeat Monday", "follow Thursday's workout", "as above", or any cross-day pointer. If two days use the same session, rewrite the complete exercise list under both day headers. The daily tracker cannot resolve those references.
   Keep each day self-contained. Post-Workout stretches belong under that same day only — never put the next day's lifts under Post-Workout / Recovery / Stretching. Do not use a lone "Recovery" or "Stretching" line between days.

8. You MAY weave a one-line daily step target into the week using Metabolic Flux Bias (BUILD-UP / HIGH FLUX raise steps above their current habit; STEADY stays modest). Do not add a Cardio, Conditioning, or Supplements section header. Full cardio is generated separately.

9. Label all 7 calendar days. Training days per week is a hard cap — program exactly that many lifting sessions, not more. Remaining days are rest or active recovery. Do not require both a dedicated recovery day AND a rest day if that would steal a training day or overflow 7 days. If they train 6 days, one rest. If 7, no extra rest day. When flux bias is BUILD-UP or HIGH FLUX, fill allowed session duration with quality work (2 to 3 working sets, 5 to 7 exercises), not extra junk sets, within hard day/duration/equipment caps.

10. Mention training intensity guidance in a natural paragraph or two rather than a formal rulebook — push close to failure on bodyweight movements since loading is limited, use tempo and pause reps to increase difficulty, and only allow a couple of controlled "cheat reps" on the last set of isolation-style moves after strict failure (never on single-leg squats, jump variations, or anything with injury risk if form breaks down). State this clearly but conversationally.

11. End the week with a short stretching/cooldown routine covering the major muscle groups trained, with rough hold times (30 to 60 seconds each side), written casually.

12. Add a short sleep note — duration target (7.5 to 9 hours) and a few practical tips suited to an Indian lifestyle (chai cutoff, screens, meal timing). Friendly suggestions, not a clinical list.

13. Close with a short, encouraging note for the week — no formal "Weekly Focus" bullet section, no rulebook-style headers, no after-week guidance.

14. Cover all 7 days. Label each day as "Day N (Weekday)" with Day 1 = Monday through Day 7 = Sunday. Include training or recovery/rest content for every day. Never skip a required training day. Balance upper and lower body across the week — don't overload either.

15. Don't mention the number of week it is, just skip mentioning it

16. **Hard constraints:** Obey the Hard Constraints section — equipment available, exercises to avoid, injuries, and training days per week are non-negotiable. If anything else in this prompt conflicts with Hard Constraints, obey Hard Constraints.

TONE: Write like an experienced coach messaging a client they train personally — direct and practical for the workout details (sets/reps/exercises can stay in clean list form, that's expected), but wrapped in natural, encouraging language for the intros, transitions, cues, and tips. Avoid robotic section headers like "DAILY STEP TARGET" or "TRAINING INTENSITY RULES" — fold that information into the flow of the plan instead.
